+//! The payload is never interpreted: a message only schedules a reload from
+//! the config database, so a rogue publisher cannot inject routes. It can
+//! still force pool rebuilds, which is why the connection authenticates
+//! (`PGDOG_NATS_USER`/`PGDOG_NATS_PASSWORD` or `PGDOG_NATS_CREDS_FILE`) and
+//! bursts are coalesced into one reload per `RELOAD_COALESCE` window.
 
